#{[17592186183982 :question/problem "In the table below, fill the corresponding dual value of Boolean function $f^d$ of $f$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f^d$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186183982 :question/worked-solution "The dual Boolean function of $f$ is the function $f^d$ defined by\n\n$$\nf^d(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) = \\lnot f(\\lnot x_1, \\lnot x_2,\\ldots, \\lnot x_n)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle f^d(0,0) = \\lnot f(\\lnot \\, 0, \\lnot \\, 0) = \\lnot f(1, 1) = \\lnot \\, 1 = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle f^d(0,1) = \\lnot f(\\lnot \\, 0, \\lnot \\, 1) = \\lnot f(1, 0) = \\lnot \\, 1 = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle f^d(1,0) = \\lnot f(\\lnot \\, 1, \\lnot \\, 0) = \\lnot f(0, 1) = \\lnot \\, 0 = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle f^d(1,1) = \\lnot f(\\lnot \\, 1, \\lnot \\, 1) = \\lnot f(0, 0) = \\lnot \\, 1 = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nNow, the completed table is presented below\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f^d$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186183984 :question/problem "Which of the Boolean functions $f_1,f_2,f_3$ given below are monotonic?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186183985 :question/worked-solution "A boolean function is monotonic if, for any comparable pair of binary tuples, we have\n\n$$\n(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\leq (y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_n) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad f(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\leq f(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_n)\n$$\n\nThe tables corresponding to our functions are the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$h$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- Function I is *not* monotonic since $(0,0) \\leq (1,0)$ but $f(0,0) > f(1,0)$.\n- Function II is monotonic. Indeed, for any comparable pair of binary tuples, we have\n$(x_1,x_2) \\leq (y_1,y_2) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad g(x_1,x_2) \\leq g(y_1,y_2)$.\nNamely:$(0,0) \\leq (0,1)$ and $g(0,0) \\leq g(0,1)$$(0,0) \\leq (1,0)$ and $g(0,0) \\leq g(1,0)$$(0,0) \\leq (1,1)$ and $g(0,0) \\leq g(1,1)$$(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$ are not comparable$(0,1) \\leq (1,1)$ and $g(0,1) \\leq g(1,1)$$(1,0) \\leq (1,1)$ and $g(1,0) \\leq g(1,1)$\n- Function III is *not* monotonic since $(0,0) \\leq (0,1)$ but $h(0,0) > h(0,1)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186183987 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the Boolean function $f$ given above. Find the Boolean polynomial corresponding to $f$ and determine whether this polynomial is affine or not."] [17592186183987 :question/worked-solution "A boolean function is affine if the corresponding Boolean polynomial for this function is linear (doesn't contain conjunctions).\n\nFirst, we construct the principal disjunctive normal form corresponding to $f$. The rows containing ones of the function and their corresponding minterms are shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\land\\overline {x_2}$</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">${x_1} \\land \\overline{x_2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$x_1 \\land {x_2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the corresponding PDNF is\n$f \\equiv (\\overline{x_1} \\land\\overline {x_2}) \\lor ({x_1} \\land \\overline{x_2})\\lor({x_1} \\land x_2)$.\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = x \\oplus 1\n$$\n\nfor each negation. Also, since each minterm in our PDNF gives $1$ on distinct combinations of variables' values, we can change all instances of $\\lor$ to $\\oplus$.\n\n$$\nf≡(x_{1}⊕1)∧(x_{2}⊕1)⊕x_{1}∧(x_{2}⊕1)⊕x_{1}∧x_{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression:\n\n- Drop the $\\land$-sign as we do with multiplication in algebraic expression to make the expressions shorter:\n$f≡(x_{1}⊕1)(x_{2}⊕1)⊕x_{1}(x_{2}⊕1)⊕x_{1}x_{2}$\n- Using logical equivalences $(A \\oplus B)C \\equiv C(A \\oplus B) \\equiv A C \\oplus B C$ and $1 \\land A \\equiv A \\land 1 \\equiv A$, we expand the parentheses:\n$f \\mid ≡x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{2}⊕x_{1}⊕1⊕x_{1}x_{2}⊕x_{1}⊕x_{1}x_{2}$\n- Using the fact that $A \\oplus B \\equiv B \\oplus A$, $A \\oplus A \\equiv 0$, and $0 \\oplus A \\equiv A$, we cancel out pairs of identical terms:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle f \\equiv  \\cancel{ {x}_{1} {x}_{2}} ⊕ {x}_{2} ⊕ \\cancel{ {x}_{1}} ⊕ 1 ⊕ \\cancel{ {x}_{1} {x}_{2}} ⊕ \\cancel{ {x}_{1}} ⊕ {x}_{1} {x}_{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  1 ⊕ {x}_{2} ⊕ {x}_{1} {x}_{2}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Boolean polynomial corresponding to $f$ is $1 \\oplus x_2 \\oplus x_1x_2$. Notice that this polynomial isn't linear, so the function $f$ isn't affine."] [17592186184000 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven the Boolean function $f$ above, fill in the missing values in the corresponding Karnaugh map for three variables below.\n\n| $\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$ | $00$ | $01$ | $1\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{I I}}}$ | $1\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{I I}}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{I I}}}$ | $0$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{I I}}}$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{I I}}}$ | $0$ |"] [17592186184000 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw an empty Karnaugh map, as shown below. Notice that the tuples corresponding to the variables $x_1x_2$ in the heading are $00$, $01$, $11$, $10$ (in that exact order). This is because any two neighboring tuples must differ from each other by exactly one place/value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe are already given some values in the map. So, let's write them down to the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's fill in the missing values in turn.\n\n- The intersection of column $01$ and row $0$ corresponds to the binary tuple $(x_1,x_2,x_3) = (0,1,0)$. From the truth table of our function, we have $f(0,1,0)=0$. So, we put $0$ in the cell located at this intersection.\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4B5\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The intersection of column $10$ and row $0$ corresponds to the binary tuple $(x_1,x_2,x_3) = (1,0,0)$. From the truth table of our function, we have $f(1,0,0)=0$. So, we put $0$ in the cell located at this intersection.\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4B5\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The intersection of column $11$ and row $1$ corresponds to the binary tuple $(x_1,x_2,x_3) = (1,1,1)$. From the truth table of our function, we have $f(1,1,1)=1$. So, we put $1$ in the cell located at this intersection.\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4B5\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the final Karnaugh map is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184001 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA Boolean function $f$ of three variables has the corresponding Karnaugh map shown above. Find a minimized DNF representing the given function."] [17592186184002 :question/problem "Consider the Boolean function $f$ given by the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFill in the blanks below corresponding to the Karnaugh map for the function $f$.\n\n| $\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$ | $00$ | $01$ | $11$ | $10$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X |}}}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, find a minimized DNF that represents the function $f$."] [17592186184002 :question/worked-solution "Comparing the column and row of each missing cell with the truth table of our function, we get the following Karnaugh map corresponding to the function:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{x_3} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{x_1x_2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo simplify a disjunctive normal form using Karnaugh maps, we need to combine $1 \\times 1$, $2 \\times 1$, $1 \\times 2$, $4 \\times 1$, $1 \\times 4$, or $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $1$'s in the map, where each block is as large as possible.\n\nHere, we have one $2 \\times 2$ block and one $1 \\times 4$ block, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7df70acadeeebc3068294df9c84dc4dde430128f2c4eaab8b44cb12d91078d0.png)\n\n- Over the $2\\times2$ block, we have $x_1=1$. So, this corresponds to $x_1$.\n- Over the $1\\times4$ block, we have $x_3=0$. So, this corresponds to $\\overline{x_3}$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following minimized expression:\n$f\\equiv x_1 \\lor \\overline{x_3}$"] [17592186184003 :question/worked-solution "In our case, the automaton has the following components:\n\n- the set of states $Q=\\{p,q,r\\}$,\n- the set of input symbols $\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}$,\n- the start state $p$, and\n- the set of final states $F=\\{q\\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's determine the transition function.\n\nFirst, we draw an empty table with a different state written in each row and a different input symbol written in each column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that we write $\\to$ next to $p$ since it's the start state, and we write $\\ast$ next to $q$ since it's a final state.\n\nNext, we fill in the remaining cells:\n\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $p$ to $p$, labeled $0$. This means that $\\delta(p,0)=p$. So, we write $p$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1$. This means that $\\delta(p,1)=q$. So, we write $q$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $1$.\n- There is an arrow from $q$ to $p$, labeled $0$. This means that $\\delta(q,0)=p$. So, we write $p$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow from $q$ to $r$, labeled $1$. This means that $\\delta(q,1)=r$. So, we write $r$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $1$.\n- There is an arrow from $r$ to $p$, labeled $0$. This means that $\\delta(r,0)=p$. So, we write $p$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $r$ to $r$, labeled $1$. This means that $\\delta(r,1)=r$. So, we write $r$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $1$.\n\nThe transition table corresponding to the given diagram is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184004 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConstruct a diagram representing the deterministic finite automaton from the transition table above."] [17592186184006 :question/worked-solution "In our case, the automaton has the following components:\n\n- the set of states $Q=\\{p,q,r\\}$,\n- the set of input symbols $\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}$,\n- the start state $p$, and\n- the set of final states $F=\\{q,r\\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's determine the transition function.\n\nFirst, we draw an empty table with a different state written in each row and a different input symbol written in each column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\:r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that we write $\\to$ next to $p$ since it's the start state, and we write $\\ast$ next to $q$ and $r$ since they are final states.\n\nNext, we fill in the remaining cells:\n\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $p$ to $p$, labeled $0$. This means that $\\delta(p,0)=\\{p\\}$. So, we write down $\\{p\\}$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $p$ to $p$, labeled $1$, and there is an arrow from $p$ to $q$, also labeled $1$. This means that $\\delta(p,1)=\\{p,q\\}$. So, we write down $\\{p,q\\}$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $1$.\n- There are no arrows from $q$ that are labeled with $0$. This means that $\\delta(q,0)=\\emptyset$. So, we write down $\\emptyset$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $q$ to $q$, labeled $1$. This means that $\\delta(q)=\\{q\\}$. So, we write down $\\{q\\}$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $1$.\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $r$ to $r$, labeled $0$, and there is an arrow from $r$ to $p$, also labeled $0$. This means that $\\delta(r,0)=\\{r,p\\}$. So, we write down $\\{r,p\\}$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $0$.\n- There are no arrows from $r$ that are labeled with $1$. This means that $\\delta(r,1)=\\emptyset$. So, we write down $\\emptyset$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $1$.\n\nThe transition table corresponding to the given diagram is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\{p\\}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\{p,q\\}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\emptyset}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\{q\\}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast\\: r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\{r,p\\}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\emptyset}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184007 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{p,q\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{q,r\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{r,p\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{r\\}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nDraw a diagram representing the nondeterministic finite automaton given by the transition table above."] [17592186184008 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! b$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{a\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{a\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{a,b\\}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the nondeterministic finite automaton represented by the transition table above. Given that $\\delta: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to 2^Q$ is the transition function of the automaton, find $\\delta(b, 001)$."] [17592186184009 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Sigma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{p\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{q\\}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{p,r\\}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\{r\\}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following strings are accepted by the finite nondeterministic automaton represented by the above diagram?\n\n- $011$\n- $0100$\n- $1101$"] [17592186184015 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2df3dbacdce4b55574884ef104f79624b4fb9a778b1503f30c6ec7be3365724.png)\n\nFill the blanks in the transition table of the Turing machine given in the diagram above.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Gamma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\square$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! q_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_1,0,L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q_0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184015 :question/worked-solution "A Turing machine is a seven-tuple of the form\n\n$$\nT = (Q, \\Sigma, \\Gamma, q_1, q_0, \\square, \\delta)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ is a nonempty finite set of states,\n- $\\Sigma$ is a nonempty finite set of input symbols,\n- $\\Gamma$ is a finite set of tape symbols with $\\Sigma \\subseteq \\Gamma$,\n- $q_1 \\in Q$ is the start state,\n- $q_0$ is the final or accepting state,\n- $\\square \\in \\Gamma \\setminus \\Sigma$ is the blank cell symbol, and\n- $\\delta$ is a transition function of the form $\\delta: Q \\times \\Gamma \\to Q \\times \\Gamma \\times \\{L, R \\}$.\n\nThe arguments of $\\delta$ are a state and a tape symbol currently scanned by the tape head. The function returns a new state, a new tape symbol replacing the old symbol in the scanned cell, and the direction in which the head moves ($L$ stands for \"left\" and $R$ stands for \"right\").\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagram.\n\n- Consider the cell at the intersection of row $q_1$ and column ${\\color{red}0}$. In the diagram, this corresponds to the loop from $q_1$ to $q_1$, labeled by ${\\color{red}0}/1,L$.![](images/99/99536a33cf4498a8dc5d7abe01f87633c7a9595dd044c1ff83981084614f1061.png)\nThis means that being in state $q_1$ and observing symbol ${\\color{red}0}$ in a cell, we stay in state $q_1$, write down $1$ into the cell, and move left. So,\n$\\delta(q_1,0) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(q_1,1,L)}$.\n\n- Consider the cell at the intersection of row $q_1$ and column ${\\color{red}\\square}$. In the diagram, this corresponds to the arrow from $q_1$ to $q_0$, labeled by ${\\color{red}\\square}/0,R$.![](images/82/82113c5de9870f7e4d6b95a5c2a5cb24d56df2bbcb9f406d43b94d37b1b53e3a.png)\nThis means that being in state $q_1$ and observing an empty cell ${\\color{red}\\square}$, we swap to state $q_0$, write down $0$ into the cell, and move right. So,\n$\\delta(q_1,\\square) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(q_0,0,R)}$.\n- Consider the cell at the intersection of row $q_0$ and column ${\\color{red}1}$. In the diagram, there are no arrows from $q_0$ with a label of the form $0$ This means that being in state $q_0$ and observing symbol ${\\color{red}1}$ in a cell, the machine gets stuck. So, $\\delta(q_0,1)$ is undefined and we write $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-}$ into the transition table.\n\nFinally, the transition table of the Turing machine takes on the following form:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Gamma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\square$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! q_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(q_1,1,L)}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_1,0,L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(q_0,0,R)}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q_0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184016 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Gamma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\square$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! q_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_1,\\square,R)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_0,1,L)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q_0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nDraw a transition diagram corresponding to the Turing machine given in the table above."] [17592186184018 :question/problem "![](images/41/413647e52f50f66e29ed75f1affe7b6b48737c3e08119378064532bbb9086bcd.png)\n\nThe string $\\square1$ is written on the tape of the Turing machine given by the transition table below, where $\\square$ is the blank symbol. The tape head initially observes the left-most symbol of the string in state $q_1$. From left to right, what are the missing parts of the tree of moves shown above for the given machine?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Gamma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\square$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! q_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_0,1,R), (q_2,\\square,R)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_0,1,R),(q_2,1,L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_0,1,L)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q_0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184019 :question/problem "![](images/ad/adc8c619d64b11217a62f1fc271be1f75220d486f5ecf41da9dc30c0ea41c7d0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a Mealy machine. Write down the correct options in the corresponding output table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\omega$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184019 :question/worked-solution "In our case, the automaton has the following components:\n\n- the set of states $Q=\\{p,q\\}$,\n- the set of input symbols $\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}$,\n- the set of output symbols $\\Gamma=\\{a,b\\}$, and\n- the start state $q$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's determine the output function.\n\nFirst, we draw an empty table with a different state written in each row and a different input symbol written in each column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\omega$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we fill in the empty cells:\n\n- There is an arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $0/\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. This means that $\\omega(p,0)=\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. So, we write $a$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $0$.\n- There is an arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $1/\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. This means that $\\omega(p,1)=\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. So, we write $a$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $1$.\n\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $q$ to $q$, labeled $0/\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. This means that $\\omega(q,0)=\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{a}$. So, we write $a$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $0$.\n\n- There is an arrow from $q$ to $p$, labeled $1/\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{b}$. This means that $\\omega(q,1)=\\bbox[3pt,Moccasin]{b}$. So, we write $b$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $1$.\n\nThe output table corresponding to the given diagram is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\omega$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184020 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\omega$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nDraw the diagram that represents the Mealy machine given by the above transition table (on the left) and the output table (on the right)?"] [17592186184021 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\omega$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the Mealy machine represented by the above transition table (on the left) and the output table (on the right). Given that $\\omega: Q \\times \\Sigma \\to \\Gamma$ is the output function of the machine, find $\\omega(p, 010)$."] [17592186184022 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0d217070d04ee1e8ba13d218c42700e8cda6e26fedde03e798171b04308b0bd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a Moore machine. Fill in the blanks in the following table corresponding to the transition function $\\delta$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184022 :question/worked-solution "In our case, the machine has the following components:\n\n- the set of states $Q=\\{p,q,r\\}$,\n- the set of input symbols $\\Sigma=\\{a, b\\}$,\n- the set of output symbols $\\Gamma=\\{a, b\\}$, and\n- the start state $p$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's determine the transition function.\n\nFirst, we draw an empty table with a different state written in each row and a different input symbol written in each column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that we write $\\to$ next to $p$ since it's the start state.\n\nNext, we fill in the cells:\n\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $p$ to $p$, labeled $a$. This means that $\\delta(p, a)=p$. So, we write $p$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $a$.\n- There is an arrow from $p$ to $q$, labeled $b$. This means that $\\delta(p,b)=q$. So, we write $q$ at the intersection of row $p$ and column $b$.\n- There is an arrow from $q$ to $p$, labeled $a$. This means that $\\delta(q,a)=p$. So, we write $p$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $a$.\n- There is an arrow from $q$ to $r$, labeled $b$. This means that $\\delta(q,b)=r$. So, we write $r$ at the intersection of row $q$ and column $b$.\n- There is an arrow from $r$ to $q$, labeled $a$. This means that $\\delta(r,a)=q$. So, we write $q$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $a$.\n- There is an arrow (loop) from $r$ to $r$, labeled $b$. This means that $\\delta(r, b)=r$. So, we write $r$ at the intersection of row $r$ and column $b$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184023 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">State</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Output $(\\omega)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConstruct a diagram representing the Moore machine given by the above transition table (on the left) and the output table (on the right)."] [17592186184024 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\delta$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$q$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">State</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Output $(\\omega)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the Moore machine represented from the (transition/output) table above. If the machine received the input string $001$, what output string would the machine return?"] [17592186184028 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ast$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\vdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ddots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\vdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\vdots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ast$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA Boolean table, such as the above, can uniquely represent a Boolean function $f$ of $3$ variables. Fill in the blanks for the reasoning about the number of Boolean functions on $3$ variables below.\n\n> Each combination of our Boolean variables can be uniquely represented by a tuple of length\n> $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$.\n\n> Now, each coordinate of the tuple can have one of $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$ possible values. Thus, by the combinatorial rule of $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{p r o d u c t}}}$, there are $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$ possible tuples in total.\n\n> As a result, our table has $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$ rows. Here, each row contains one of $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$ possible function values. Therefore, by the combinatorial rule of $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{p r o d u c t}}}$, there are $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{X X}}}$ possible functions in total."] [17592186184029 :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the truth tables for $\\lor$ and $\\oplus{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1 \\lor x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1 \\oplus x_2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that the table's $3$rd and $4$th columns are the same. The only difference is in the $4$th row.\n\nWe established that $\\lor$ and $\\oplus$ are almost equivalent. So, we could use these symbols interchangeably if our expression does not allow the disjunction of two $1$'s. But this is precisely the case for any principal disjunctive normal form (PDNF):\nsince each conjunction of literals in a PDNF equals $1$ only on one combination of Boolean variables, in a PDNF, we will never have a disjunction of $1$'s.\n\nFinally, we conclude that we can change any occurrence of $\\lor$ to $\\oplus$ in any PDNF.\n\n**Watch out!** We can't swap $\\lor$ and $\\oplus$ in an arbitrary expression; here, we only proved this could be done in PDNFs."] [17592186184030 :question/problem "Complete the missing values in the truth table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1 \\downarrow x_2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184030 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{True}=1$ and $\\textrm{False}=0.$ \n\nAlso, the Peirce arrow $x_1 \\downarrow x_2$ is true if and only if both $x_1$ and $x_2$ are false ($0$).\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Boolean function is the following: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1 \\downarrow x_2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186184072 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle f ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 x + 4}{ {x}^{2} + x - 2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 ( x + 2 )}{( x + 2 ) ( x - 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\cancel{( x + 2 )}}{\\cancel{( x + 2 )} ( x - 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{x - 1}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = 1$,\n- therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\">Approaching $1$ (from the <em>left</em>) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$200$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 1^-$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$"] [17592186184073 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$.\n\nFirst, let's factor the function and cancel the common factors (if any):\n\n$$\nArgument for \\require is not a valid package name\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the *left*.\n- Similarly, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the *right*.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $2$ using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">Approaching $2$$\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Approaching $2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.01$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now analyze each of the statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The values of $f(x)$ in our table are *negative* for $x < 2$, and they *decrease* as we approach $x=2$ from the *left*. Therefore,\n$f(x)\\to {\\color{red}{-\\infty}} \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 2^{-}$\n- Statement II is true. The values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive* for $x >2$, and they *increase* as we approach $x=2$ from the *right.*\n$f(x)\\to {\\color{blue}{\\infty}} \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 2^{+}$\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true yet statement I is false, we conclude that\n$f(x)\\nrightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186184118 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $5$ from the *right*.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors (if any), we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle f ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 x + 10}{25 - {x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 ( x + 5 )}{( 5 + x ) ( 5 - x )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\cancel{( x + 5 )}}{\\cancel{( 5 + x )} ( 5 - x )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5 - x}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = 5$,\n- therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $5$ from the right.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $5$ from the right using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Approaching $5$ (from the <em>right</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.0001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-20\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-200$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *negative*, and they *decrease* as we approach $x = 5$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}{-\\infty}}$ as $x \\rightarrow 5^+$, which means that\n\n$$\na = {\\color{red}{-\\infty}}\n$$"] [17592186184119 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $3$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$,\n- therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*,\n- similarly, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $3$ using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">Approaching $3$$\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Approaching $3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.01$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-350.75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3500.75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3499.25$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$349.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now analyze each of the statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The values of $f(x)$ in our table are *negative* for $x < 3$, and they *decrease* as we approach $x=3$ from the *left*.\nTherefore,\n$f(x)\\to {\\color{red}{-\\infty}} \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 3^{-}$.\n- Statement II is true. The values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive* for $x >3$, and they *increase* as we approach $x= 3$ from the *right*.\n$f(x)\\to {\\color{blue}{\\infty}} \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 3^{+}$\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true yet statement I is false, we conclude that\n$f(x) \\nrightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186184562 :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be Boolean functions of two variables defined as\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x_{1}, x_{2}) = x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}, g(x_{1}, x_{2}) = (x_{2} + 1)mod2.\n$$\nComplete the following table corresponding to the function $g(x_{1}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}))$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x_{1}, f(x_{1}, x_{2}))$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHint: If $x$ is an integer, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle x \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , & \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{if } x \\text{ is even,} \\\\ 1 , & \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{if } x \\text{ is odd} .\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186184564