ata-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12\n$$\n\nis less than $13$, we subtract $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4}$ from $13$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can't subtract any further since $\\fbox{1}$ is less than $3$. Hence, $1$ is the remainder."] [17592186188046 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\textrm{Var}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=1\\big]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $\\mu_{Y|1} = \\dfrac{9}{4}.$"] [17592186188046 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{2}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$\\dfrac{3}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+1+\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{81}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188064 :question/worked-solution "To write a two-digit number (greater than $20$) in words, we\n\n- write the tens part, followed by a hyphen\n- then, write the ones part.\n\nLet's write down the place values of $81$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we write the number in words:\n\n- The number has $\\color{blue}8$ tens, so we write \"eighty,\" followed by a hyphen:$\\qquad$\"*eighty-*\"\n- The number has $\\color{red}1$ ones, so we add \"one\": $\\qquad$ \"*eighty-one*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $81$ written in words is *eighty-one*."] [17592186188068 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y].$"] [17592186188068 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space $S_X,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.4-{1.8}^{2} \\\\  & =2.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space $S_Y,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+3\\cdot 0\\cdot f(3,0)+3\\cdot 2\\cdot f(3,2) \\\\  & =0+0+0+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.2-1.8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X+2Y] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 2.16+{2}^{2}\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 2\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\  & =54+4-12 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188070 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 3$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [17592186188070 :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n$$\n\\Sigma = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\text{Var} [ X ] & \\text{Cov} [ X , Y ] \\\\ \\text{Cov} [ Y , X ] & \\text{Var} [ Y ]\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support $S_X,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =10-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support $S_Y,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =5.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =5.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\textrm{E}[XY]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(4)(1)f(4,1)+(2)(3)f(2,3)+(4)(3)f(4,3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.1+6\\cdot 0.2+12\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =7-3\\cdot 2.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n$$\n\\Sigma\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\text{Var} [ X ] & \\text{Cov} [ X , Y ] \\\\ \\text{Cov} [ Y , X ] & \\text{Var} [ Y ]\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.96\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [17592186188080 :question/worked-solution "First, we break down the number \"five hundred thirty-two\":\n\n- \"five hundred\" means the number has $\\color{blue}5$ hundreds\n- \"thirty\" means the number has $\\color{red}3$ tens\n- \"two\" means the number has $\\color{purple}2$ ones\n\nNow, we write the values in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{purple}2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"five hundred thirty-two\" written using digits is $532$."] [17592186188090 :question/worked-solution "Let's write \"$7$ thousands and $3$ tens\" in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"$7$ thousands and $3$ tens\" is equivalent to \"$7,030$ ones.\""] [17592186188091 :question/worked-solution "Let's write the number $4,700$ in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"$4,700$ ones\" is equivalent to \"$4$ thousands and $7$ hundreds.\""] [17592186188109 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *tens* place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}4$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $25,234$ rounded to the nearest ten is $25,230$."] [17592186188110 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *hundreds* place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *tens* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}3$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the hundreds (into tens and ones):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $200,238$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $200,200$."] [17592186188111 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *thousands* place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}4$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $7,452$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $7,000$."] [17592186188154 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *tens* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}5$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place and increase the *tens* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *tens* is the same as $1$ *hundred* and $0$ *tens*. So, we must write $0$ in the *tens* place and increase the number of *hundreds* by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $311,695$ rounded to the nearest ten is $311,700$."] [17592186188155 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *thousands* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}8$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones) and increase the *thousands* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *thousands* is the same as $1$ *ten thousand* and $0$ *thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *thousands* place and increase the number of *ten-thousands* by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $9,821$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $10,000$."] [17592186188156 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *ten-thousands* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *thousands* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>millions</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}7$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow three steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the ten-thousands (into thousands, hundreds, tens, and ones) and increase the *ten-thousands* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>millions</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *ten-thousands* is the same as $1$ *hundred thousand* and $0$ *ten-thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *ten-thousands* place and increase the number of *hundred-thousands* by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>millions</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- But, $9+1=10$ *hundred-thousands* is the same as $1$ *million* and $0$ *hundred-thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *hundred-thousands* place and increase the number of *millions* by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>millions</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $2,997,523$ rounded to the nearest ten thousand is $3,000,000$."] [17592186188177 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *tens* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" 