ass=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $54,385$ rounded to the nearest ten is $54,390$."] [17592186188178 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *thousands* place.\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}8$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ into all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Then, we increase the *thousands* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $7,825$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $8,000$."] [17592186188179 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2\\,\\text{kg} & =0.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.2\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =200\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $200 \\,\\text{g}.$"] [17592186188180 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $2.25 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.25\\,\\text{g} & =2.25\\,\\text{g}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\text{g}} \\\\  & =2.25\\,\\cancel{\\text{g}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{g}}} \\\\  & =2.25\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{mg} \\\\  & =2\\,250\\,\\text{mg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.25 \\,\\textrm{g}$ is equivalent to $2\\,250\\,\\text{mg}.$"] [17592186188181 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 15 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{km}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\cdot  1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $15\\,000\\,\\textrm m$."] [17592186188182 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm g)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{kg}),$ we need to multiply  $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{1000\\,\\textrm g}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,300\\,\\text{g} & =1\\,300\\,\\text{g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}} \\\\  & =1\\,300\\,\\cancel{\\text{g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\cancel{\\text{g}}} \\\\  & =1\\,300\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}}{1000} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,300}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{kg} \\\\  & =1.3\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ is equivalent to $1.3 \\,\\text{kg}.$"] [17592186188183 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $4.5\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 4.5 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} & \\displaystyle  = 4.5 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{10 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm}}{\\text{cm}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.5 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{cm}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{10 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.5 \\cdot  10 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 45 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4.5\\,\\textrm{cm}$ is equivalent to $45 \\,\\textrm{mm}$."] [17592186188185 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{m})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{km})$, we need to multiply $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 800 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 800 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 800 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{m}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\hspace{0.167em} 800 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 800}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ is equivalent to $1.8\\,\\textrm{km}$."] [17592186188187 :question/worked-solution "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{cm})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{m})$, we need to multiply $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 4 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{cm}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{cm}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{100}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{100}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 40 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ is equivalent to $40\\,\\textrm{m}$."] [17592186188190 :question/problem "A study aims to assess the average scores of a population of $N=676$ students in a particular school district. They took the scores of a sample of $n=52$ students. Given that the population mean score is $\\mu=75\\%$ with population standard deviation $\\sigma=15$, what is the probability that the mean score of the students in the sample is more than $70\\%$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0062 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0060 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0059 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0057 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0082 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0080 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0078 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0075 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.3 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0107 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0104 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0102 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.0099 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188192 :question/problem "A total of $N=400$ students use an e-learning platform. In a random sample of $n=130$ of these students, $78$ declared they were satisfied with the quality of the course materials.\n\nFind a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of students satisfied with the course materials.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188192 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all students subscribed to the e-learning platform,\n- some of the students are satisfied with the quality of the course materials, some aren't,\n- the size of the random sample is $n=130$,\n- the size of the population is $N=400$, and\n- the proportion of students from the sample who are satisfied can be found as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{78}{130} = 0.6\n$$\n\nNow, we have that $n=130$, $N=400$, and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6\n$$\n\nso\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 130\\cdot 0.6= 78> 5$,\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 130\\cdot (1-0.6) = 52> 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use a normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size.\n\nNotice that the finite population correction factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis necessary when the sample size is a significant proportion of the population, typically exceeding $5\\%\\cdot N$. In our case, we have\n\n$$\n130> 5\\%\\cdot N = 5\\%\\cdot 400 = 20\n$$\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/95/95a498945e3bc43a2e40ebc2d31206c4b49afba182bf0453a72e1e7201d0a8b4.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.05}=1.645{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is\n\n$$\n0.6 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.6(1 - 0.6))/(130) \\cdot \\frac{400 - 130}{400 - 1}}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n0.6 ± 0.058\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n(0.6- 0.058, \\, 0.6+ 0.058) = (0.54, 0.66)\n$$"] [17592186188194 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.07 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9292 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9306 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9319 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9418 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9429 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9441 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9525 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9535 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9545 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188195 :question/problem "A popular streaming service has a database of $100$ million songs, of which $64$ million have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. If a user randomly creates a playlist with $128$ songs, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $64$ and $96$ (inclusive) songs in the sample have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.3 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.2 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0006 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.07 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9949 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9951 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9952 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9962 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9963 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9964 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.7 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9972 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9973 \\,$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9974 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188196 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have $4$ in the ten-thousands place. So the complete statement is as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number $47, 683$ has $4$ ten-thousands.*"] [17592186188211 :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\textrm{E}[4X-3Y].$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188211 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,  the probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p></p></th>\n<th></th>\n<th>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.35+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =-0.35+0.7+1.2 \\\\  & =1.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.2+5\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0+0.2+0.6+1.5 \\\\  & =2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X-3Y] & =4\\text{E}[X]-3\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4(1.55)-3(2.3) \\\\  & =6.2-6.9 \\\\  & =-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188213 :question/problem "In a library, each shelf is filled with $20$ volumes of an encyclopedia, plus one bookend. The weight of one volume, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\textrm g,$ while the weight of a bookend, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\textrm g.$ If one shelf is randomly selected, what is the probability that it supports a weight of more than $3\\,000\\,\\textrm g?$ You may assume that the weights of the volumes and bookend are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.0 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9772 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9778 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9783 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9788 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.1 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9821 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9826 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9830 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9834 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.2 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9861 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9864 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9868 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9871 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188536 :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186188537 :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[5X-2Y].$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-al